  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 314 AM EST Mon Nov 23 2026\n\nThis morning's mid-level water vapor imagery reveals moisture \nassociated with a longwave trough dropping down over Minnesota's \nArrowhead and rotating through the UP. RAP surface analysis shows \nnicely kinked isobars through the center of the UP demarking a zone \nof convergence, which has helped to focus snowfall thus far tonight. \nNorth of a line connecting roughly Ontonagon, Big Bay, and Sault Ste \nMarie, observations show northerly surface flow; south of this line, \nwesterly flow prevails. The rain-to-snow transition is well under \nway, with the Keweenaw and areas west and interior seeing \ntemperatures up as low as 28F, while much of the eastern UP is still \nseeing mid 30s. Webcams show accumulations of at least an inch \nextending east from Ontonagon County through southern Houghton to \nMichigamme. While the radar has indicated a steady precipitation \nband streaming over the western UP, clipping the southern half of \nthe Keweenaw, for much of the night, this band is showing signs of \ndissipating in the west as it becomes more cellular in nature and \nreturns decrease. Winds remain somewhat elevated, with some spots \nwith westerly flow consistently gusting to 25 mph through the night, \nand northerly flow filling in a bit stronger while gusting to 25-30 \nmph. \n\nThe time of this writing (2 AM EST) is roughly when high-\nresolution short-term guidance indicates tonight's banding \nshould dissipate as the trough drops south through the CWA and \nflow becomes more uniformly northerly. Lake effect snow will \ncontinue in multiple smaller bands along the west to northwest \nwind belts, given continued favorable thermodynamics: \nsaturation or high humidity and plenty of lift through the DGZ,\ninversion heights reaching up to 15kft, and several hundred J/kg\nof lake induced CAPE. As 850mb temperatures drop to -14C later\ntoday, surface high temperatures are expected to achieve only\nthe low 30s (mid 30s near lakeshores) before falling into the\nteens and low 20s tonight. Likewise, SLRs decrease throughout\nthe day to 16:1 in higher elevations (mostly in the west) and\n12:1 in lower areas, and snow stands higher chances of\naccumulating on cooler ground surfaces. In conjunction with a\nsecond impulse moving south through the area this afternoon, a\nfew models are showing a convergent band signal focused on Alger\nCounty, though with less magnitude and consensus than the\nwestern band already observed tonight. This band, and continued\nareas in west/northwest snow belts, show areas of 20-40% chances\nof visibility than 1 mile throughout the day today and into the\nevening due to higher snowfall rates. Additional snow totals\ninclude 3-5\" in the west (Gogebic, Ontonagon, southern Houghton\nand northwestern Iron Counties) and in western Alger County, \n1-3\" elsewhere in the northerly/northwesterly snow belts, and \nless than 1\" along Lake Superior shorelines. \n\nDrier air starts moving into the mid-levels in western parts of\nthe CWA later this morning, gradually working down to the low- \nlevels by evening (with timing a little bit later for the east).\nWith lake effect cloud cover giving way Monday night, \ntemperatures look to bottom out in the teens in the interior \nwest, mid 20s along Great Lakes shorelines, and low 20s \nelsewhere. Temperature-wise, with ridging continuing overhead, \nTuesday and Tuesday night look to be a rinse and repeat of \nMonday and Monday night, which places things at nearly normal \nfor highs Monday and Tuesday and 0-10F below normal during the \nnights. A lifting low pressure system glances the area late \nThanksgiving Day into Friday, with 10-20% chances of \nprecipitation in the south and far east.",
